{"title":"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find, rounded to the nearest multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the sum of all fractions in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f159909a995f3eead00a6fa1f41b5bd1b8264a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2024 + 24x}{2025 + 25x},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"98\" height=\"40\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is some nonnegative integer, such that when fully simplified, the denominator is exactly one more than the numerator.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/b/31b8226f323c3087826e7ea0f17e824b2f67ae33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 8 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) }12 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 16 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 20 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"392\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When a fraction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/4/084079bf1d54500f3c59257a7ec3467932b72ba2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a}{b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"34\" /> is fully simplified the numerator and denominator are both divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/eda0b9382f30f58a3f6f1159bb606dcbce1e8914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(a,b).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" /> So we can rewrite the problem's condition as: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/f/9afc45430eb3705b3fc721a3bb276b532182a4f9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2024 + 24x}{\\gcd(2024 + 24x, 2025 + 25x)} + 1 = \\frac{2025 + 25x}{\\gcd(2024 + 24x, 2025 + 25x)}.\\]\" width=\"512\" height=\"42\" />\nFirst, simplify the gcd using the Euclidean Algorithm: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/5/59578524e2aed8870f3651da3190785e19d16901.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\gcd(2024 + 24x, 2025 + 25x) = \\gcd(2024 + 24x - 24(2025 + 25x - 2024 - 24x), 2025 + 25x - 2024 - 24x) = \\gcd(2000, x + 1)\\]\" width=\"893\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Now we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/3/7d3142b8936ca097ceef2606b3b53e0916f71a9a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2024 + 24x}{\\gcd(2000, x + 1)} + 1 = \\frac{2025 + 25x}{\\gcd(2000, x + 1)} \\implies \\gcd(2000, x + 1) = x + 1 \\implies \\text{x + 1 must be a factor of 2000}.\\]\" width=\"812\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>The prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/b/6ebf98050fdb773529d45a834b87ae5818a5397d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^4 \\cdot 5^3,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"20\" /> so it has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/3/6632aace93dab72df0ad144c84c805de391c2143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4 + 1) \\cdot (3 + 1) = 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"166\" height=\"20\" /> factors. Since the numerator and denominator differ by only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/f/5bf27edd6ee0c25241392922eca9b9c9144f4d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"17\" /> each fraction will approximately equal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdd1b53e5faf7a118222e4b7571d1e9afa0cf56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> fractions (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> possible values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43b6bcde0e66de78dde6250138abf955e45c4ed5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />), their approximate sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc8df0638f63dacee5341e69c0ebb91a512cfc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 20}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"72\" height=\"30\" />\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018041102\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.014 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.078 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 67/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3635/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36067:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018041102 and revision id 279821. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}